
╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║  NanoLang v0.6 - Self-Hosted Compiler                       ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Usage: nanoc <input.nano> [-o output] [options]

Options:
  -o <file>        Explicit native output; --emit-nvm selects module output
  -v, --verbose    Verbose compilation output
  -k, --keep-c     Keep generated C file
  --target <t>    native or c; unqualified compilation publishes input.nvm
  --emit-nvm     I publish verified NanoISA after VM shadow checks; requires -o
  --test-imports  I run dependency shadows before root shadows (default)
  --root-shadows-only  I run only root-file shadows
  --allow-websocket-connections I grant WebSocket shadows outbound connections
  --allow-websocket-lookup I separately grant DNS with --websocket-resolver-helper PATH
  --allow-tcp-connections I grant selected TCP shadows outbound connections
  --allow-temporary-files  I grant selected File shadows temporary-file access
  File graphs default to native output (a.out without -o); runtime grants are separate
  --              End options; the remaining argument is my input path
  -fshow-intermediate-code  Print generated C to stdout
  -h, --help       Show this help
  --llm-diags-json <p>  Write machine-readable diagnostics JSON (agent-only)

This compiler is 100% written in NanoLang!
Pipeline: Lex → Parse → Typecheck → NanoISA → optional native translation

